<p>Water losses are some of the most common, and most disputed, property claims in North Texas. If you are filing a <strong>water damage insurance claim in Dallas</strong>, the questions that matter most are where the water came from, how suddenly it happened, and how far it spread before it was stopped. A burst pipe after a hard freeze, a cracked washing machine hose in a Plano two-story, a slab leak under a Lake Highlands ranch, or rain driven through a hail-damaged roof in Garland can all produce similar-looking stains. Coverage, however, can be very different for each one. Dallas Public Adjuster helps homeowners and business owners document water losses carefully so the claim reflects what actually happened.</p>

<h2>Common Sources of Water Damage in Dallas Homes and Buildings</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Burst and frozen pipes.</strong> Winter Storm Uri in February 2021 brought single-digit lows to Dallas County, and pipes in attics, exterior walls, and pier-and-beam crawlspaces split across the area. Later ice storms in February 2023, January 2025, and January 2026 caused more freeze losses. See <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Roof leaks after hail or wind.</strong> When hail or wind damages shingles, flashing, or a low-slope membrane, rain can follow. Many policies cover the interior damage when a covered peril first creates the opening. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Plumbing and appliance failures.</strong> Supply lines, water heaters, dishwashers, ice makers, and toilet connections can fail without warning.</li>
<li><strong>Slab leaks.</strong> Most Dallas homes sit on slab-on-grade foundations, and leaks in pipes under the slab can soak flooring and walls before anyone notices.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC condensate and drain lines.</strong> Attic air handlers that overflow during long Texas summers can stain ceilings and soak insulation.</li>
<li><strong>Mold-related secondary damage.</strong> Water left in walls, cabinets, and subfloors can lead to mold, which many policies limit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sudden and Accidental vs. Long-Term Seepage</h2>
<p>Most homeowners and commercial policies distinguish between a sudden, accidental discharge of water and a leak that went on for weeks or months. The first is often covered. The second is often excluded as seepage, deterioration, or wear and tear. Insurers look at clues such as rot, rust, layered staining, and mold growth to decide which side of that line a claim falls on.</p>
<p>That makes early evidence important. A plumber’s written description of the failed part, photos of the break, the date you first noticed water, and moisture readings taken by the mitigation crew can all help show the loss was recent and sudden. If the insurer concludes otherwise, ask for the specific policy language and the facts it relied on in writing.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Are-Public-Adjusters-Worth-It-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster with a clipboard and a couple inspecting a water-damaged ceiling inside a home" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Interior water damage often reaches further than the visible stain, so the inspection should follow the water path.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Flood vs. Water Damage: Why the Source Matters</h2>
<p>Dallas sees intense rain. During the August 21–22, 2022 flash flood, parts of Dallas received upwards of 15 inches of rain, and water entered homes and apartments in east Dallas, Mesquite, and Balch Springs. Standard property policies generally exclude flood, meaning surface water that rises and enters the building from outside. That coverage typically comes from a separate NFIP or private flood policy, which you can learn about at <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FloodSmart.gov</a>.</p>
<p>Rain that enters through an opening created by wind or hail is different, and it is often handled under the property policy. When a single storm produces both a roof leak and rising water, documenting each source separately matters. Our <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> page explains how those claims differ.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take After a Water Loss</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop the water.</strong> Shut off the main valve to the building. Inside the City of Dallas, water service is provided by Dallas Water Utilities; suburban cities have their own water departments. Learn where your shutoff is before you need it.</li>
<li><strong>Make the property safe.</strong> Turn off electricity to wet areas if it is safe to do so.</li>
<li><strong>Document before cleanup.</strong> Take wide photos and close-ups of the source, standing water, wet materials, and damaged contents. Video walkthroughs help.</li>
<li><strong>Start mitigation.</strong> Most policies require reasonable steps to prevent further damage. Ask the mitigation company for moisture maps, readings, and daily drying logs.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the failed part.</strong> Save the burst pipe section, supply line, or water heater fitting. It may be key evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly.</strong> Note the claim number, adjuster name, and every conversation. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help you stay organized.</li>
</ol>

