<h2>What Insurers Commonly Miss on Water Claims</h2>
<p>Water travels along framing, under flooring, and behind cabinets. Initial estimates often focus on the visible ceiling stain or the room where the leak started, and leave out the rest. Items that are frequently missing or underpriced include:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Commonly overlooked items</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Flooring</td><td>Continuous flooring that runs into undamaged rooms, underlayment, pad, and baseboards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walls and ceilings</td><td>Insulation, drywall above the flood cut, texture matching, and paint to a natural break</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cabinets and counters</td><td>Toe-kicks, cabinet backs, and countertops that must be detached to replace damaged bases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access and tear-out</td><td>Opening walls, slab, or floors to reach a covered leak, where the policy provides it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contents and living costs</td><td>Damaged belongings, pack-out and storage, and additional living expenses if the home is unlivable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mitigation</td><td>Equipment days, antimicrobial treatment, and contents manipulation</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If the insurer’s estimate does not match the scope of the damage, an independent <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can show line by line where the numbers differ.</p>

<h2>Dallas Construction Details That Affect Water Claims</h2>
<p>Older pier-and-beam homes in Lakewood, the M Streets, and Oak Cliff have exposed plumbing in crawlspaces that is vulnerable in hard freezes, and water can damage subfloors and joists that are easy to miss from inside. Slab homes across Richardson, Mesquite, and Garland can hide leaks under tile or wood flooring. Commercial buildings, apartment complexes, and warehouses with flat TPO or modified-bitumen roofs can let water travel a long way before it shows up on a ceiling. Each of those details changes how the inspection should be done.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster in Dallas Can Help</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents you, not the insurance company. On a water loss, that can mean reviewing your policy for water, mold, and access coverage, tracing the water path through the building, documenting the damage with photos and Matterport 3D imaging where useful, and preparing a detailed Xactimate estimate. We then present the claim to the insurer and handle the back-and-forth that follows.</p>
<p>We cannot promise a particular result, and coverage always depends on the policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If you are unsure whether your water loss is covered, start with a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
